Stop selecting a worker once it loses readiness, selector membership,
termination, or a verifiable current state, and make that withdrawal identity-correct
and reversible.

THE DEFECT, in four parts that hide each other. The Kubernetes watcher watches all Pods
and applies the label selector in a client-side filter
(src/service_discovery.rs): the stream was consumed through applied_objects() and then
passed through a filter_map that dropped any Pod not matching the selector. A Pod that
loses the eligibility label therefore never reached the handler at all -- a filter can
only discard objects, and a withdrawal is exactly the case where the object is still
there. Second, the same filter_map discarded the Delete event and the Init and
InitDone arms of the relist. What applied_objects() actually does is decode an Event
into the object it carries and, with emit_deleted false, pass Apply and InitApply
through while discarding Delete, Init and InitDone: upstream therefore still saw each
object of a relist, and never saw the point at which the watcher states that an object
previously Applied is now absent, which is why an abruptly removed worker stayed
registered for the life of the process. Third, handle_pod_event had a branch for a
healthy Pod and no branch at all for an unhealthy one, so even a Pod that did arrive
with readiness lost was silently ignored, and the deletion-timestamp branch that
upstream did have was reachable only after a PodInfo had been rebuilt from the current
object.

Behind all three sat a fourth problem that made a partial fix useless rather than
wrong. The tracker was a HashSet<PodInfo> and PodInfo derives PartialEq, Eq and Hash
over the whole struct, including is_ready, status and ip. A Ready -> NotReady
transition, a phase change or an address change all produce a PodInfo that no longer
compares equal to the registered one, so any removal looked up by the current PodInfo
would MISS the live entry and the worker would stay in the pool; and because the
address is part of the key, a same-name replacement that reused an address aliased the
previous identity.

THE FIX. The watch stream is consumed as raw Events and the selector decision moved
into the handler, where the Pod itself is in hand: a Pod that is terminating, no longer
matches the selectors, or is present but not healthy is a withdrawal. Termination is
checked FIRST, before any status is parsed and before selector membership, because a
terminating Pod can still report Ready=True and Running, and reading health first would
re-register it and leave the withdrawal to the final Delete event -- the preStop path
this replaces. Apply and Delete are handled directly, and the relist is implemented as
the contract describes: Init opens a set, InitApply records UIDs, InitDone withdraws
every tracked UID the listing did not contain.

The tracker becomes a HashMap keyed on the Pod UID, and the stored value is the PodInfo
the worker was REGISTERED with rather than as it is now, so a withdrawal issued for an
old identity removes that old URL and role. A UID-keyed map alone does not make that
true at the router, because the router is keyed by URL: if a new UID were admitted at
an address the old UID still held, the map would carry both, and a later Delete for the
old UID would remove the CURRENT occupant from the pool. register_worker therefore
reconciles address ownership before admitting a new UID -- any other UID registered at
the same address is withdrawn first, and a failed withdrawal holds the new add -- so an
address has exactly one owner and a late delete for a replaced UID finds nothing to act
on. A Pod whose UID is absent OR EMPTY is refused rather than admitted, because an
empty string is not an identity: it is the one value every unidentified Pod shares, so
it would alias unrelated Pods into one registration and make a later withdrawal remove
the wrong worker.

A Pod that still matches the selectors but whose current status or address cannot be
read is WITHDRAWN, not retained. Eligibility is not health, and a registration that
cannot be shown healthy must not stay selectable.

A registration is dropped only after the router has actually been told, and a failure
returns false rather than being swallowed. The watcher loop treats that false as a
reason to end the stream, so the existing backoff recreates the watcher and the relist
that comes with it reconciles whatever is still held. That is the only retry added here:
no second timer, no polling loop, and the failed withdrawal is not left waiting for a
Pod event that may never arrive. The backoff is taken alone on that path rather than
followed by the full check-interval sleep, which would push the retry past any sensible
withdrawal budget. The first error is what is logged, on every attempt.

P/D behaviour is preserved: the add and remove paths keep the existing VllmPDRouter
prefill/decode routing, bootstrap port handling and the regular-mode fallbacks, and are
only reached from the new decision points.

AGAINST: vllm-project/router v0.1.15 (commit
1fbcde7443d75b36befb61bc081f64c2a1f13a4b), the pin named by
VLLM_ROUTER_VERSION in pack/llm-router/Dockerfile. The image build applies this patch
with git apply from the clone root, which fails loudly on a pin whose hunks no longer
match, so a version bump that absorbs or breaks the patch is detected at build time
rather than in a cluster.

The added tests drive the discovery decisions through handle_watch_event and assert on
the tracker and the router: readiness loss withdraws while a healthy observation of the
same identity does not; a label deselect withdraws; a terminating Pod withdraws both
while reporting Ready and with no status at all; a relist withdraws an absent UID and
keeps a listed one; a new UID takes an address over from its previous owner, and a late
delete for the replaced UID then cannot reach the current owner; an eligible Pod whose
current status is unusable withdraws; an absent or empty UID is refused; a withdrawal
drops only the exact registration it removed; a delete for an untracked UID is a
delivered no-op.

The successful-insert leg is not asserted here: Router::add_worker polls the worker's
health endpoint, so a registration that is genuinely added cannot be observed with the
in-crate test router. The full add/withdraw/re-add round trip, and the ordering of a
replacement against a late delete, are measured against the built binary on placement
evidence in the operator's event-matrix harness rather than claimed from a unit test.

